1
00:00:00,622 --> 00:00:08,292
Hello and welcome to Coffee and Control, the podcast where I find fascinating people from within the world of control and convince them to answer all of my questions.

2
00:00:09,198 --> 00:00:19,844
I'm your host, Lucy Hudgins, and this episode is a little bit different from my regular podcast because last month I attended the conference on decision and control in the beautiful city of Rio de Janeiro.

3
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:24,320
And whilst I was there, I went to a ton of different talks, met some very wonderful people.

4
00:00:24,686 --> 00:00:26,960
This is just going to be a snippet of CDC.

5
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:33,840
There were way more talks that I didn't manage to see than those that I did, and there were talks that I loved that didn't make it into this episode.

6
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:36,644
But hopefully it gives you a little taste of what it was like.

7
00:00:37,038 --> 00:00:55,460
So get ready to hear about fresh and groundbreaking research on a vast array of topics, including control architecture, formal verification, extremum seeking, reinforcement learning, equitable mobility systems, and a whole lot more as you join me on a journey to the Conference of Decision and Control 2025.

8
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:35,716
I've already seen a lot of other fellow control engineers going to CDC.

9
00:01:36,238 --> 00:01:38,320
Also, Rio has been very nice.

10
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:46,320
I have spent the last week in Rio itself doing a lot of sightseeing and running and cycling and swimming, which has been very lovely.

11
00:01:46,574 --> 00:01:53,124
I'm about to go to conference, hopefully, talk to lots of lovely people about their research.

12
00:01:53,806 --> 00:01:54,308
Let's go.

13
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:57,360
So I've just checked in to CDC.

14
00:01:57,550 --> 00:01:59,120
Um, it's very exciting.

15
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:09,840
But the thing that I wanted to mention was that in the corner there is loads of posterboards showcasing different historical female influences in automatic control.

16
00:02:10,157 --> 00:02:13,440
So this was put together by researchers at London University.

17
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:17,840
Um and it I like I just love that this is a thing.

18
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,316
So there are I think maybe nine or ten different women in control who I had never heard of.

19
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,400
Why like why have I never heard of any of these women?

20
00:02:28,718 --> 00:02:38,596
So examples of these women include Nina Thornhill, Basenna Pasek Duncan, Imgod Flugelutz, and Maria Domenico Di Benedetto, just to name a few.

21
00:02:38,958 --> 00:02:47,332
And if you'd like to learn more about these women, the In Control podcast has just released an episode on exactly this topic, which I would definitely recommend listening to.

22
00:02:47,726 --> 00:02:58,372
Also, the team that created these banners have a survey where they're trying to collect data related to diversity within control, both in terms of gender but also kind of goals and perspectives of researchers working in the field.

23
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:03,600
So I will leave a link in the description if you'd be interested in filling this out to help them with their research.

24
00:03:03,950 --> 00:03:10,640
But yes, I do really love that people are starting to highlight the fascinating and amazing women in our field.

25
00:03:11,086 --> 00:03:13,156
Okay, I think workshops start pretty soon.

26
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:14,720
So see you later.

27
00:03:15,246 --> 00:03:16,480
My name is Alessandro Baate.

28
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:28,960
I work at Oxford University Computer Science on control and verification of complex engineering systems, in particular AI systems.

29
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:38,800
So our idea is to use techniques from control and verification to certify, to guarantee AI systems.

30
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:43,760
I've given a talk titled Neural Proofs.

31
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:55,040
This is a sound approach to synthetize certificate functions which are neural shaped, neural templated.

32
00:03:55,342 --> 00:04:17,279
Such functions are synthesized according to proof rules, which are requirements on the dynamics of the complex system of interest, and such proofs allow to certify that a system abides by complex requirements, any particular memory-dependent, and no Markovian requirements.

33
00:04:17,934 --> 00:04:36,160
These proofs can be characterized on the dynamics of the model and on the specification, and can be practically synthesized with an approach that blends machine learning, training neural networks, with a generalization step that a SAT modular theory solver does.

34
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:42,800
So we are able to provide sound guarantees, sound certificates that are learned from data.

35
00:04:43,566 --> 00:04:51,428
So a SAT solver is a program used to try to establish whether a particular formula involving Boolean variables is satisfiable.

36
00:04:51,758 --> 00:05:01,636
And Alessandra's talk fits into the broader picture of formal verification, which involves using mathematical reasoning and proof to verify that a system behaves as intended.

37
00:05:02,094 --> 00:05:11,268
There are actually a ton of different talks on formal verification at CDC, including the PR lecture on the first proper day of the conference, which we'll get onto a little later in the episode.

38
00:05:12,334 --> 00:05:13,376
My name is Megan Tucker.

39
00:05:13,536 --> 00:05:24,096
I'm an assistant professor at Georgia Tech. A summary of the talk would be that I think it's important for us to consider the layered architecture for personalizing human robot systems.

40
00:05:24,496 --> 00:05:31,776
At the highest level, we want to be able to personalize these systems, to be able to account for humans' individual needs, but we still want our systems to be safe.

41
00:05:31,856 --> 00:05:38,336
And so at the lowest level, we need to make sure that our safety critical systems have the ability to be personalized at that high level.

42
00:05:38,496 --> 00:05:39,616
And so what does that look like?

43
00:05:39,696 --> 00:05:40,496
How do we do that?

44
00:05:40,736 --> 00:05:42,336
That was the essence of the talk.

45
00:05:43,086 --> 00:05:47,696
So it's currently early afternoon of the first day of the conference.

46
00:05:47,856 --> 00:05:55,456
So this morning I went into a session on stochastic systems, stochastic planning and control, which was very interesting.

47
00:05:55,536 --> 00:06:00,176
And then I went into a workshop on control theory architecture, which was so cool.

48
00:06:00,366 --> 00:06:05,296
Um, I think every single one of the talks in that session was just fascinating.

49
00:06:05,456 --> 00:06:10,896
After that, a load of people from the control theory architecture session went out for lunch, so I went with them.

50
00:06:11,056 --> 00:06:12,896
Um it was kind of chaos.

51
00:06:12,976 --> 00:06:20,868
I think the little shack opposite the conference venue was very much not prepared for a bunch of control engineers just descending on it wanting food.

52
00:06:21,390 --> 00:06:22,576
but we did get served.

53
00:06:22,766 --> 00:06:23,376
It was very nice.

54
00:06:23,456 --> 00:06:31,456
It was very lovely chatting to all of the speakers and other PhD students in a very chill environment.

55
00:06:32,014 --> 00:06:35,748
My name is Joe Moeller and I'm a postdoc at Caltech.

56
00:06:36,736 --> 00:06:50,916
And my talk was about using category theory to generalize Lyapunov stability theory to broader classes of system, including hybrid systems.

57
00:06:52,430 --> 00:06:57,188
So category theory refers to a general theory of mathematical structures and their relations.

58
00:06:57,456 --> 00:07:06,916
And I didn't know this when I spoke to him, but it turns out that Joe has written a series of blog posts about category theory, which I would definitely recommend checking out if you would like to learn all about this.

59
00:07:07,726 --> 00:07:11,652
Now, another talk given in this workshop session was by Rodolf Zapolka.

60
00:07:12,016 --> 00:07:17,936
So I did manage to get an interview with him, but unfortunately I did not set up the microphones correctly, so it didn't record properly.

61
00:07:18,016 --> 00:07:23,136
So I'm going to try and give a little summary here about what that talk was about because I did really enjoy it.

62
00:07:23,502 --> 00:07:34,212
Essentially, Rodolf's work combines continuous and discrete control through spiking event-based control systems, where you have things happening when a particular continuous signal spikes above a certain level.

63
00:07:34,670 --> 00:07:43,616
And Rodolph had previously used this to control a pendulum, but this talk discussed how to scale this up to be able to apply it to control a soft robotic snake.

64
00:07:44,014 --> 00:07:51,456
So the architecture that he used combined rebound excitability, which is a phenomenon that shows up in neuroscience, with a winner-takes-all network.

65
00:07:51,566 --> 00:07:56,516
And Rudolph and his collaborators were able to show that this structure is hierarchical.

66
00:07:56,656 --> 00:08:02,896
So you can combine instances of these networks together, and the resulting network exhibits the same kind of behaviors.

67
00:08:03,406 --> 00:08:04,416
I've linked the paper below.

68
00:08:04,496 --> 00:08:11,076
You can also listen to one of the early in control episodes where Rodolf goes into a lot more detail on his work on spike and control systems.

69
00:08:12,078 --> 00:08:12,976
Hi, I'm Raj.

70
00:08:13,216 --> 00:08:17,936
I am a PhD student in the ECE department at University of Illinois, Urbana Champaign.

71
00:08:18,096 --> 00:08:20,176
I work on decision making under uncertainty.

72
00:08:20,256 --> 00:08:26,736
And when you think of decision making under uncertainty, most of the times you think of you model uncertainty as something stochastic.

73
00:08:26,896 --> 00:08:34,467
But it often can be the case where the uncertainty is not stochastic, but it is generated by some other agent with some other incentives in mind.

74
00:08:34,895 --> 00:08:45,668
So my research tries to think about what kind of information structures arise in the equilibrium in this particular game between the information provider and the person taking decisions under uncertainty.

75
00:08:46,096 --> 00:08:51,776
And I think one of the cool things that I got to do with my research was actually test this on human subjects and see what happens.

76
00:08:51,936 --> 00:09:01,776
And do people change their decisions when they know that the information is being provided by someone strategic compared to the information just being there and it's stochastic?

77
00:09:01,936 --> 00:09:04,516
And I mean, like the teaser is they do change.

78
00:09:04,782 --> 00:09:07,812
So it matters on who's giving the information and what are their incentives.

79
00:09:08,416 --> 00:09:08,736
Hello.

80
00:09:08,816 --> 00:09:14,628
Uh my name is Vijay, uh and I'm a PhD student at the University of Illinois, Albana Champaign.

81
00:09:15,296 --> 00:09:28,132
And uh my research revolves around what is called uh information design, which is the game theoretic concept that uh or the game theoretic study rather of how best to share information across multiple strategic agents.

82
00:09:28,896 --> 00:09:39,492
And the focus of my work sort of is this that if you don't know how the other person that you're talking to is going to respond to the information that you're sharing with them, how do you learn how to share information with them?

83
00:09:39,854 --> 00:09:45,856
And I think the key element here is that when you are trying to learn something, you're typically trying out very different ways.

84
00:09:45,936 --> 00:09:47,936
You're exploring your strategy space.

85
00:09:48,176 --> 00:10:00,256
And what that would mean in this context of information design is that the other person that sees you trying out very different strategies, uh using very different information sharing strategies, would think something's wrong with you.

86
00:10:00,416 --> 00:10:00,576
Right?

87
00:10:00,736 --> 00:10:05,220
They would think that you're crazy and you keep saying a different thing at every every second step.

88
00:10:05,806 --> 00:10:17,540
So my research tries to incorporate this into the model of how you can learn in this type of a setup by assuming the receivers that are getting this information have some type of a memory.

89
00:10:18,894 --> 00:10:21,764
So it's currently the end of the workshops on the first day of the conference.

90
00:10:21,856 --> 00:10:24,740
Um I'm kind of going between different sessions this afternoon.

91
00:10:25,358 --> 00:10:28,176
Um I've just come out of a session on game theory.

92
00:10:28,336 --> 00:10:33,696
Also, I had a long conversation with John Doyle, which was unexpected.

93
00:10:33,776 --> 00:10:43,776
Um and he was kind of telling me about his recent research on cancer control and also um kind of architecture of social systems, which was very interesting.

94
00:10:44,014 --> 00:10:46,436
Um, was not expecting that interaction this afternoon.

95
00:10:47,342 --> 00:10:54,896
for anyone who doesn't know who John Doyle is, he made huge advances in robust control in the 1980s and 90s and has done a ton of interesting work since then.

96
00:10:55,182 --> 00:10:57,456
I've left a link below to his in control interview.

97
00:10:57,536 --> 00:11:02,096
If you'd like to find out more about his work, he is an absolute legend in the field.

98
00:11:02,222 --> 00:11:09,284
Um, and I actually ended up sitting next to him in the control theory architecture workshop and was then very excited to actually get to speak to him later on.

99
00:11:09,518 --> 00:11:22,276
And that's actually one of the things that I really love about control conferences is that you get to meet these people that are like super famous within the field of control, and then you talk to them and you realize that they're just like normal people and are actually really friendly and approachable.

100
00:11:48,014 --> 00:11:59,856
Okay, so it's currently um about nine o'clock on the uh first proper day of the conference, so I went to the pre-relecture this morning, which was interesting.

101
00:12:00,016 --> 00:12:11,076
It was on formal verification language and then using that for distributed systems, um, and also links between that and control barrier functions.

102
00:12:11,616 --> 00:12:21,456
Um I was not really concentrating because I have my talk in about an hour and a half um and I am kind of nervous about it.

103
00:12:21,838 --> 00:12:28,416
Okay, so whilst I was not entirely tuned into focusing on the plenary lecture at the time, it was live streamed and uploaded to YouTube.

104
00:12:28,622 --> 00:12:31,296
So I'm going to leave a link below to the full video.

105
00:12:31,534 --> 00:12:33,924
But here's a little bit of a taste of what it was actually like.

106
00:12:34,382 --> 00:12:43,844
So my name is Dimos Di Marogos, I work at KTH, and I'm going to talk about using transient controllers to satisfy high-level multi-robot tasks.

107
00:12:44,654 --> 00:13:06,660
So, my first statement for today is that while there has been a focus in the last 10 years on end-to-end approaches, I think current use cases and complexity of tasks score more and more for real-timeness, reactivity, reproducibility, repeatability of experiments, and thus feedback becomes again.

108
00:13:06,976 --> 00:13:07,684
essential.

109
00:13:08,558 --> 00:13:21,252
The general theme I'm going to talk about today involves how can we design controllers that enhance these three features in multi agent systems, distributed feedback and task complexity.

110
00:13:22,816 --> 00:13:31,108
So there have been tremendous research efforts in multi-agent control, mainly using graph theoretic approaches.

111
00:13:31,566 --> 00:13:40,164
But I argue that mainly the focus has been in our community on simple cooperative control objectives such as formation and consensus.

112
00:13:40,976 --> 00:13:49,604
So around 2012, I put the question to myself: how can we ask a robotic system to perform more complex tasks?

113
00:13:49,856 --> 00:13:52,964
How can we get more interesting things from a robotic system?

114
00:13:53,390 --> 00:14:07,296
And back then I became acquainted with this literature in robotics that use formal verification languages to define such complex tasks and then derives controllers based on tools from computer science.

115
00:14:07,616 --> 00:14:09,536
Okay, so it's currently four o'clock.

116
00:14:09,646 --> 00:14:16,016
I haven't been able to attend that many talks today because I've been doing long form interviews for future episodes.

117
00:14:16,416 --> 00:14:24,096
however, I have just attended a session on adaptive control, which there were a few really interesting talks.

118
00:14:24,256 --> 00:14:31,696
So one of them, which I really enjoyed, was on the impact of attacks on controlling multi-agent systems and how to deal with that.

119
00:14:31,790 --> 00:14:32,736
I really enjoyed that.

120
00:14:32,816 --> 00:14:40,496
And then there was another one on extremum seeking control inspired by insect wing flapping, which I thought was a really cool idea.

121
00:14:40,816 --> 00:14:43,376
Also, my talk this morning, I think went well.

122
00:14:43,502 --> 00:14:46,256
I definitely talked too quickly, um, but it was fun.

123
00:14:46,638 --> 00:14:54,500
So, for anyone who might be interested, my CDC talk was on a framework for applying multiple model iterative learning control to nonlinear systems.

124
00:14:55,118 --> 00:15:12,896
So essentially, this framework is designed for systems with very large model uncertainties, and instead of using a single model to design your ILC controller, you use a set of potential candidate models, and between each iteration, you identify the model that's closest to your true system and use that for control.

125
00:15:13,454 --> 00:15:28,708
Essentially, this paper was adapting and extending existing theory for linear systems to the nonlinear case, and the reason that we wanted to do this was that I'm hoping to apply this framework in the context of stroke rehabilitation, which is what I'm mostly doing my PhD research in.

126
00:15:30,126 --> 00:15:32,976
And there were also some other really interesting talks.

127
00:15:33,390 --> 00:15:39,856
So actually, the first presenter was Alberto Isidori, which was a bit insane to be presenting in the same session as him.

128
00:15:40,014 --> 00:15:42,336
And also he watched my talk, which was mad.

129
00:15:42,606 --> 00:16:01,456
So the session that my talk was in was on nonlinear control, which is basically a combination of like control barrier function-based approaches and kind of classical control or like stabilization of nonlinear systems, and then um I officer this afternoon.

130
00:16:01,616 --> 00:16:11,376
I'm trying to figure out whether I'm going to go into the session on extreme seeking control or one of the sessions on biological systems, control of biological systems.

131
00:16:11,694 --> 00:16:17,456
I'm Viktoria Gryzkowska from University of Klagenfurt in Austria, quite far away from here.

132
00:16:17,776 --> 00:16:25,216
And in this talk, I presented the results of our joint work with uh Same Heiser from University of Cincinnati.

133
00:16:25,456 --> 00:16:39,456
And we were working on extremum-seeking problem, which means that we need to optimize some function without knowing actually the analytical expression of this function, but just using some measurements of this function.

134
00:16:39,616 --> 00:16:40,816
And yeah.

135
00:16:40,976 --> 00:16:53,216
so in this talk, we presented some approach, which is based on higher order lee brackets, which allows us to get access to higher order derivatives of the cost function.

136
00:16:53,376 --> 00:17:03,776
and with this, we obtained a better decay rate, namely exponential decay rate for polynomial cost functions with degree greater than quadratic one.

137
00:17:04,685 --> 00:17:16,164
So at the most basic level, a lee bracket is an operator that takes two vector fields f and g and returns a third vector field corresponding to the derivative of g along the flow generated by f.

138
00:17:16,526 --> 00:17:31,748
And you can generalize this concept to arbitrary numbers of vector fields fi by calculating the le bracket of f1 and f2, then taking the output of this and calculating the lee bracket between this and f3, and then continuing this for as many vector fields as you're interested in.

139
00:17:33,166 --> 00:17:36,900
So I'm Jorge Poreira, I'm an associate professor at UC San Diego.

140
00:17:37,456 --> 00:17:42,308
And I presented some work on using event trigger control for extreme syncing dynamics.

141
00:17:43,182 --> 00:17:58,276
So the main idea is to study how we can save some infrastructure when you are running these adaptive algorithms and you don't want to use these resources all the time, maybe on the input or on the output of the system, but you just want to perhaps sample or actuate based on events.

142
00:17:58,958 --> 00:18:01,456
So this is a well-known technique, you know, event trigger control.

143
00:18:01,776 --> 00:18:05,796
So we just study how to formalize event trigger control into extreme seeking systems.

144
00:18:06,896 --> 00:18:12,816
and to do that, we use tools from hybrid dynamical systems, because usually event trigger systems are hybrid systems.

145
00:18:12,976 --> 00:18:17,156
You have an interaction between continuous and dynamics and descriptive and dynamics.

146
00:18:18,030 --> 00:18:20,100
This leads to close-up systems that are hybrid.

147
00:18:20,496 --> 00:18:26,404
And the key idea in extremo seeking control is to use perturbation theory to solve model free optimization problems.

148
00:18:27,214 --> 00:18:34,820
So the key problem to study is how to use this perturbation theory for hybrid systems to prove stability guarantees for the proposed dynamics.

149
00:18:35,438 --> 00:18:36,176
And that's what we did.

150
00:18:36,256 --> 00:18:49,060
So we showed that we can actually design well-posed event trigger algorithms, establish some stability results, well-posedness in terms of solutions, existence, completeness, absence of xeno behavior.

151
00:18:49,550 --> 00:18:57,764
Um, we did that for extremely seeking dynamics that use classic algorithm theory as well as for those that use leave-acted out in theory.

152
00:18:58,574 --> 00:19:00,964
And we show that both can be studied using tools from hybrid systems.

153
00:19:02,382 --> 00:19:06,416
So I've just come out of a session on extremum seeking control, which was very interesting.

154
00:19:06,496 --> 00:19:18,976
And now I'm just about to go to the Meet the Candidates poster session, which is designed for students or postdocs that are looking for faculty positions to kind of show off their research.

155
00:19:19,600 --> 00:19:27,492
But I'm hoping this is a good chance to kind of find out interesting things that various different people are doing and maybe get audio clips from them.

156
00:19:28,686 --> 00:19:31,376
So the Meet the Candidates poster session ended up being so much fun.

157
00:19:31,456 --> 00:19:43,376
I met so many interesting and super smart researchers working on a wide variety of different topics, including network estimation, physics informed learning, traffic control, game theory, and a loadable.

158
00:19:43,886 --> 00:19:51,172
I actually decided that I would take the audio I collected and put it into a separate mini episode that's going to be released at some point in the next few weeks.

159
00:19:51,296 --> 00:19:52,228
So look out for that.

160
00:20:29,296 --> 00:20:31,480
Okay, my name is also Zaglar.

161
00:20:31,856 --> 00:20:39,296
I'm a professor at Massachusetts Institute of Technology in the Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Department.

162
00:20:40,336 --> 00:20:51,696
so the talk really was about uh you know how can we think about AI agents making decisions in dynamic environments.

163
00:20:52,016 --> 00:20:53,776
and it has multiple dimensions.

164
00:20:53,936 --> 00:21:03,056
This is about how do agents learn about the environment, about other agents that they are interacting with.

165
00:21:03,136 --> 00:21:20,256
So I'm really focusing on multi-agent settings because when you think about AI agents, um you know most promising applications really are thinking about agents being personalized assistance to humans or multiple AI agents collaborating to solve complex tasks together.

166
00:21:20,336 --> 00:21:32,816
So it's really important to understand how in these multi-agent scenarios, these agents learn both about the unknown environment and about each other and how they make good decisions.

167
00:21:32,976 --> 00:21:37,696
And you know, an important aspect of that is of course human-AI interaction.

168
00:21:37,936 --> 00:21:48,816
it's something that I think attracts a lot of attention lately because of the huge advances in AI and fears around AI replacing humans.

169
00:21:49,776 --> 00:22:06,816
there is, I think, a sort of paradigm in AI development that focuses a lot on AGI, artificial general intelligence, which tries to sort of make superhuman AI agents, AI agents that surpass humans.

170
00:22:07,216 --> 00:22:08,416
That's not my vision.

171
00:22:08,576 --> 00:22:16,656
I feel like the development should be how humans and AI work better together, how AI can enhance human flourishing.

172
00:22:16,816 --> 00:22:26,816
So that's sort of the view we're taking and trying to understand how can we design better AI systems that can actually augment humans, make better decisions.

173
00:22:26,976 --> 00:22:31,536
And the talk was about sort of thinking about a systematic framework for doing that.

174
00:22:32,622 --> 00:22:39,172
So after the plenary lecture, I attended half of a session on robust control and then half of one on control system architecture.

175
00:22:39,886 --> 00:22:42,884
There were two super interesting talks in the robust control session.

176
00:22:43,088 --> 00:22:46,436
So one was by Matt Turner, who I've interviewed on the podcast before.

177
00:22:46,608 --> 00:22:51,844
He was talking about how to define L2 game bounds for systems with quantized inputs.

178
00:22:52,270 --> 00:22:55,088
And then there was another talk given by Professor Kemin Zhu.

179
00:22:55,328 --> 00:23:02,528
