00:40:52,238 --> 00:40:56,400
The next part gets a little technical, and I wanted to clarify a key term up front.

343
00:40:56,560 --> 00:41:02,820
So a semi-simple eigenvalue is one for which geometric and algebraic multiplicity coincide.

344
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:09,604
So the number of times it appears as a root of the characteristic polynomial is the same as the dimension of its eigen space.

345
00:41:10,542 --> 00:41:20,068
So for a single integrated system with a Laplacian L, you want it to be equivalent to a system where you don't have the Laplacian.

346
00:41:20,240 --> 00:41:20,932
So it's mixed.

347
00:41:22,766 --> 00:41:24,708
That's not straightforward how to do that.

348
00:41:25,422 --> 00:41:30,820
Because the L itself is usually has a semi-simple eigenvalue in zero.

349
00:41:31,918 --> 00:41:36,516
So separating the semi-simple part of the system does the trick.

350
00:41:37,294 --> 00:41:41,956
First apply generalized balancing then to the asymptotically stable part of the system.

351
00:41:42,414 --> 00:41:48,548
Generalized balancing, so you have to pick the gradients wisely, and then transform it to a Laplacian.

352
00:41:49,838 --> 00:41:52,708
And add the semi-simple part back.

353
00:41:54,446 --> 00:41:58,628
So it gives a guaranteed a priori error bound if you do it in this way.

