364
00:42:51,840 --> 00:43:04,100
So the approach we came up with is a path following double loop scheme where we have a auxiliary problem that we try to solve in a inner loop.

365
00:43:04,720 --> 00:43:09,444
And then in the outer loop, we update our parameters.

366
00:43:10,160 --> 00:43:15,396
And with updating the parameters, also the auxiliary problem changes.

367
00:43:15,790 --> 00:43:21,508
So then we go back to the inner loop and the new auxiliary problem is being solved.

368
00:43:21,600 --> 00:43:33,572
And then we were able to show on the one hand that our inner loop algorithm without a stopping criterion is converging to the solution of the auxiliary problem.

369
00:43:34,400 --> 00:43:48,240
And then we also showed that our outer loop is weakly converging with like an ergodic convergence type of result to a solution of the HVI problem.

370
00:43:48,320 --> 00:43:52,480
So of the both levels combined problem.

371
00:43:52,960 --> 00:44:06,884
And the nice thing about the approach, or what is probably like the most important result that we derived, is that we were able to get convergence rates, which is not that common for these kind of problems yet.

